.TH dircolors 1 (date) "GNU coreutils (unreleased)"
.SH NAME
dircolors \- color setup for ls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY dircolors
.RI [ OPTION \~.\|.\|.\&]
.RI [ FILE ]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\" Add any additional description here
.PP
Output commands to set the LS_COLORS environment variable.
.SS "Determine format of output:"
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#dircolors\-b
\fB\-b, \-\-sh, \-\-bourne\-shell\fP
.UE
output Bourne shell code to set LS_COLORS
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#dircolors\-c
\fB\-c, \-\-csh, \-\-c\-shell\fP
.UE
output C shell code to set LS_COLORS
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#dircolors\-p
\fB\-p, \-\-print\-database\fP
.UE
output defaults
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/coreutils.html#dircolors\-\-print\-ls\-colors
\fB\-\-print\-ls\-colors\fP
.UE
output fully escaped colors for display
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/dircolors#dircolors\-\-help
\fB\-\-help\fP
.UE
display this help and exit
.TP
.UR https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/dircolors#dircolors\-\-version
\fB\-\-version\fP
.UE
output version information and exit
.PP
If FILE is specified, read it to determine which colors to use for which
file types and extensions.  Otherwise, a precompiled database is used.
For details on the format of these files, run 'dircolors \fB\-\-print\-database\fR'.
